onebrain vendor patch 0003: RPC client-side async pipelining
(docs/perf.md §3 — overlapped chunked prefill)

Applies on top of 0001 + 0002. Client paths of ggml-rpc.cpp (plus one
additive declaration in ggml-rpc.h), two small hunks in llama-context.cpp,
and one hunk in ggml-backend.cpp; the server side and the wire format are
untouched.

Mechanism (details at the ob_rpc_pending block in ggml-rpc.cpp): the RPC
server executes commands serially, in order, per connection, and several
commands carry no response (GRAPH_COMPUTE, GRAPH_RECOMPUTE, SET_TENSOR,
MEMSET_TENSOR). Deferring "acks" is therefore free: the response of ANY
later response-bearing command on the same socket proves every command
before it fully executed. The patch adds

 - a per-socket pending-command ledger (submitted/completed counters, in
   commands AND wire bytes — the pending-ack FIFO with implicit, batched
   acks);
 - pipelined fences ("ack tickets"): event_record puts one
   GET_DEVICE_MEMORY REQUEST on the wire immediately and defers reading
   the response; a later event wait pops tickets in FIFO order, so
   draining to an old marker never waits for work submitted after it.
   Without tickets, a wait-side fence can only wait for the WHOLE queue,
   which re-serializes a deep ubatch train to depth one;
 - client-side flow control: the unproven in-flight wire bytes are capped
   at roughly FOUR ubatches of traffic — the scheduler's own rotation
   depth (adaptive: 4.5x the largest command of the current burst +
   1 MiB, floor 4 MiB, OB_RPC_INFLIGHT_CAP overrides, 0 disables; the
   learned size resets whenever the ledger fully drains so load-time
   weight pushes never inflate it). Measured root cause of the unbounded
   collapse (2.5x SLOWER than sequential on the CI netem leg, bimodal):
   the RPC bytes ride a QUIC tunnel, and with stock net.core.rmem_max
   (212992 B ~= 1.7 ms of 1 Gbit) the receiver's kernel UDP buffer
   overflows whenever QUIC receive processing falls behind line-rate
   arrival — routine while the same host computes a graph. Every
   overflow is packet loss to QUIC (netstat -su RcvbufErrors; the netem
   qdisc dropped zero packets) and loss recovery collapses the
   congestion window. The cap bounds the standing backlog and with it
   the damage of any one loss episode, while measuring within a few
   percent of uncapped on a healthy path. Over-cap sends first pop a
   ticket, i.e. block until the server has executed one whole earlier
   ubatch;
 - synchronize as drain-everything; events as markers into the ledger;
   async set/get/cpy tensor entry points;
 - a client-side cache for GET_ALLOC_SIZE responses (resolves upstream's
   own TODO): graph allocation queries alloc sizes per ubatch
   (FLASH_ATTN_EXT nodes), and any response-bearing round trip must first
   drain every outstanding ticket — cached, steady state issues no
   allocation round trips at all;
 - a stable non-NULL sentinel from buffer_get_base on a dead socket
   (patches/0002 gap): a model can outlive its bridge, and the next
   session's setup walks existing buffers — NULL escalated into
   upstream's generic GGML_ASSERT(base != NULL) abort. An RPC base is a
   REMOTE address used only for pointer arithmetic, never dereferenced
   locally, and every later command on the dead socket fails fast, so a
   well-aligned sentinel is safe and the failure surfaces as the typed
   session-creation error instead of an abort;
 - device caps flip to async=true, events=true — exactly what llama.cpp's
   pipeline_parallel gate requires; ggml_backend_rpc_pipeline_enable(false)
   reverts the caps advertisement process-wide (the hook behind OneBrain's
   `[perf] prefill_overlap = false` constructed M3 baseline).

The ggml-backend.cpp hunk skips the scheduler's split-input copy for
empty tensors (a zero in ne — e.g. a graph that gathers 0 output rows
during chunked prefill): there is no data and no dependency, yet the
synchronizing fallback stalled the scheduler on the producing backend
once per ubatch for a zero-byte copy, serializing pipeline-parallel
prefill end to end. Upstream-worthy on its own.

The llama-context.cpp hunks:
 1. resolve upstream's TODO at the pipeline_parallel gate ("should we
    ignore ACCEL types too?"): ACCEL devices (BLAS/Accelerate — linked
    into our macOS CPU builds) advertise no async/events and vetoed
    pipelining for the whole context; ggml_backend_sched already
    null-checks per-backend events and falls back to blocking
    synchronization, so an eventless ACCEL backend simply runs its own
    boundaries synchronously while capable backends still overlap.
 2. keep graph REUSE off the pipelined path (multi-token ubatches under
    pipeline parallelism): the reuse path skips
    ggml_backend_sched_alloc_graph — the scheduler's pipeline copies
    never rotate — and must fully synchronize before set_inputs (the
    in-flight graph reads the tensors being overwritten), so a "reused"
    prefill executes strictly sequentially per ubatch. Multi-token
    ubatches take the alloc path (rotating copies, real overlap);
    single-token decode keeps reuse — one ubatch per llama_decode has
    nothing to overlap with, and the rebuild would cost latency for no
    gain.

Failures compose with 0002: a failed fence/ticket read marks the socket
dead and the error surfaces on whichever status-returning call drains or
runs next; teardown drains naturally through the response-bearing
FREE_BUFFER round trips (live server) or fails fast (dead socket).

